  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 7,
    "lesson_title": "Energy Signals vs. Power Signals",
    "lesson_core_move": "Classify continuous-time signals as energy signals, power signals, or neither by using total energy and time-average power calculations to apply the finite-energy, finite-nonzero-power, and neither-case criteria in the Signal Size module.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Energy Signals vs. Power Signals",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Energy Signals vs. Power Signals",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Classify a continuous-time signal by its total energy and its time-average power. Do not decide from the shape alone; decide from the two calculations.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "E_x=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}|x(t)|^2\\,dt\n\nP_x=\\lim_{T\\to\\infty}\\frac{1}{2T}\\int_{-T}^{T}|x(t)|^2\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "These two quantities measure different ideas of size. $E_x$ accumulates the signal's squared magnitude over all time, while $P_x$ measures the long-run average squared magnitude. The cleanest base case is a finite-duration nonzero pulse: it has finite, nonzero energy and zero average power.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "0<E_x<\\infty \\Rightarrow \\text{energy signal}\n\n0<P_x<\\infty \\Rightarrow \\text{power signal}\n\n\\text{otherwise} \\Rightarrow \\text{neither}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use that rule exactly as written: finite nonzero energy gives an energy signal, finite nonzero average power gives a power signal, and anything else is neither. The worked examples in this topic will test one finite-energy case, one finite-power case, and two neither cases.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Energy Signals vs. Power Signals",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Classifying Signals with Finite Nonzero Energy",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Classify $x(t)=e^{-t}u(t)$ as an energy signal, a power signal, or neither by computing total energy and average power.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a one-sided decaying transient: $x(t)=e^{-t}u(t)$ is nonzero for $t\\ge 0$ and decays as $t$ increases. Use the energy and power definitions from the introduction, and keep the notation unchanged.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "E_x=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}|x(t)|^2\\,dt=\\int_0^\\infty e^{-2t}\\,dt=\\frac12",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That result is finite and nonzero, so the signal meets the energy-signal criterion. The remaining check is the long-run average power.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "P_x=\\lim_{T\\to\\infty}\\frac{1}{2T}\\int_{-T}^{T}|x(t)|^2\\,dt=\\lim_{T\\to\\infty}\\frac{1}{2T}\\int_0^T e^{-2t}\\,dt=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Since $0<E_x<\\infty$ and $P_x=0$, this signal is an energy signal, not a power signal. The decaying transient has finite total energy even though it extends forever to the right.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Energy Signals vs. Power Signals",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Classifying Signals with Finite Nonzero Average Power",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Classify $x(t)=\\cos(\\omega_0 t)$ as an energy signal, a power signal, or neither by computing total energy and average power.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a bounded periodic waveform: $x(t)=\\cos(\\omega_0 t)$ never turns off, so the right test is average power, not finite-duration energy. Use the definitions from the introduction and keep the notation unchanged.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "P_x=\\lim_{T\\to\\infty}\\frac{1}{2T}\\int_{-T}^{T}\\cos^2(\\omega_0 t)\\,dt=\\frac12",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That value is finite and nonzero, so the signal satisfies the power-signal criterion. The remaining check is total energy.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "E_x=\\lim_{N\\to\\infty}\\int_0^{N\\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega_0}}\\cos^2(\\omega_0 t)\\,dt=\\lim_{N\\to\\infty}N\\frac{\\pi}{\\omega_0}=\\infty",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Since $0<P_x<\\infty$ and $E_x=\\infty$, this signal is a power signal, not an energy signal. A non-decaying periodic waveform can therefore have finite average power even though its total energy diverges.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Energy Signals vs. Power Signals",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Classifying Signals with Infinite Energy and Infinite Average Power",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Classify $x(t)=t\\,u(t)$ as an energy signal, a power signal, or neither by computing total energy and average power.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a growing causal signal: $x(t)=t\\,u(t)$ is zero for $t<0$ and then increases without bound. The shape alone is not enough, so compute both $E_x$ and $P_x$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "E_x=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}|x(t)|^2\\,dt=\\int_0^\\infty t^2\\,dt=\\infty",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Since the energy integral diverges, the signal cannot be an energy signal. The remaining test is the long-run average power.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "P_x=\\lim_{T\\to\\infty}\\frac{1}{2T}\\int_{-T}^{T}|x(t)|^2\\,dt=\\lim_{T\\to\\infty}\\frac{1}{2T}\\int_0^T t^2\\,dt=\\lim_{T\\to\\infty}\\frac{T^2}{6}=\\infty",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because $E_x=\\infty$ and $P_x=\\infty$, the signal is neither an energy signal nor a power signal. A growing signal can fail both criteria at once.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Energy Signals vs. Power Signals",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Classifying Signals with Infinite Energy and Zero Average Power",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Classify $x(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}}u(t)$ as an energy signal, a power signal, or neither by computing total energy and average power.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a slowly decaying causal tail: $x(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}}u(t)$ is nonzero for $t\\ge 0$ and shrinks slowly enough that the energy test is not obvious from the shape alone. Compute both $E_x$ and $P_x$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "E_x=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}|x(t)|^2\\,dt=\\int_0^\\infty \\frac{1}{t+1}\\,dt=\\infty",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Since the energy integral diverges logarithmically, the signal cannot be an energy signal. The remaining test is the long-run average power.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "P_x=\\lim_{T\\to\\infty}\\frac{1}{2T}\\int_{-T}^{T}|x(t)|^2\\,dt=\\lim_{T\\to\\infty}\\frac{1}{2T}\\int_0^T \\frac{1}{t+1}\\,dt=\\lim_{T\\to\\infty}\\frac{\\ln(T+1)}{2T}=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because $E_x=\\infty$ and $P_x=0$, the signal is neither an energy signal nor a power signal. Slow decay is not enough to make the energy finite, and zero average power does not override infinite energy.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
